## Introduction
Owing to their light weight, high durability, and low-costs, plastics are ubiquitous in consumer goods worldwide. However, with a rapid generation rate and low recycling rate, the majority of plastic accumulates in landfills and natural environments (Figure 1A)[1] Recently, interest in developing routes to convert plastic waste polymers into either monomers or value-added products has grown tremendously with the ultimate goal of achieving a circular economy in the polymer life cycle.[2] In particular, polyethylene terephthalate (PET), which is typically used to produce fibers, beverage bottles, and food packaging, presents a prime target as it is produced at an annual scale of almost 70 million tons worldwide,[3] highlighting the need for effective recycling strategies for this polymer. Although PET can be thermomechanically processed and repurposed following its initial use, high temperatures required to melt PET afford lower grade polymers with inferior properties that ultimately limit this strategy to only a handful of cycles.[4-5] Alternatively, breaking down PET into its monomeric components via chemical[5-6] or biotechnological[7] processing holds promise for closing the loop in a circular PET economy since isolating these products in high purity would enable the regeneration of high quality PET products. While biotechnological plastic degradation has gained increasing attention recently, it is still far from industrial application due to poor thermostability and low activity of the catalysts, as well as short enzyme lifetimes and difficulties related to enzyme recovery and reuse;[8] therefore, chemical recycling is considered the most promising method for application in industry at this point in time.[8d,
Unfortunately, some common chemical processes for PET degradation, such as hydrolysis, methanolysis and glycolysis, require the use of extensive solvent and degradation agents, high reaction pressure and oligomeric side products emerging after these processes are often laborious to separate.[5] Moreover, the recycling process is more challenging if the PET is dyed due to the formation of discolored degradation products.[4] Recently, catalytic hydrogenolysis has emerged as an attractive strategy to deconstruct PET. For example, some ruthenium complexes can degrade PET under high ${\sf H}_{2}$ pressures to afford the corresponding alcohols.[10] Additionally, a ${\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{M}}{\mathsf{o}}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ composite catalyst enables the deconstruction of PET into its monomer, terephthalic acid (TA), under 1 bar ${\sf H}_{2}$ .[11] More recently, a bimetallic catalyst CoMo@NC was also employed to deconstruct the PET via hydrogenolysis.[12] However, these catalytic composites are either too sensitive and degrade under ambient conditions, or their disordered structures hinder detailed analysis of the structural changes that the catalyst undergoes during the reaction. For industrial scale catalytic processes, high conversion and selectivity for the desired product, as well as high stability and reusability of the catalyst, are perhaps the most important performance parameters, and long-range order is not necessarily required to achieve excellent performance. However, in terms of catalyst development, amorphous catalysts that lack long-range order can present challenges during structural characterization, which can hinder detailed studies into the catalytic mechanism. Additionally, compared to amorphous or ill-defined catalysts, crystalline catalysts with well-defined structures usually contain evenly distributed active sites, which not only benefits the understanding of the catalytic process, but also potentially offers routes to improve the efficiency and selectivity of the catalytic sites through rational design of both the catalyst structures and the active sites of the catalysts. Thus, an ideal PET deconstruction catalyst should exhibit both good reactivity and a structure with long-range order to allow for structure-activity studies, which may serve as guidance for the design of next-generation polymer degradation catalysts.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), assembled from metal-based nodes and organic linkers, comprise one such class of materials and have been widely investigated because of their highly ordered structures, ultrahigh surface areas, permanent porosity with tunable pore apertures, and adjustable chemical functionality.[13] In particular, the Brønsted and Lewis acid sites on the metal nodes of some MOFs have been shown to catalyze reactions that include hydrolysis, oxidation, dehydration, and esterification.[14] Among all MOFs, zirconium-based MOFs (Zr-MOFs) have attracted widespread interest due to their relatively high thermal and chemical stability[15] and the versatility of catalyzing a wide range of chemical transformations[15a]. For example, our group previously demonstrated that unsaturated metal acid sites on the $Z r_{6}O_{8}$ nodes in the Zr-MOF NU-1000 could successfully catalyze olefin hydrogenation and isomerization[16]. In this study, we expand on this observed reactivity and interrogate whether ZrMOFs can provide an effective platform for deconstructing polyester-based plastic PET.
To probe this hypothesis, we employed the Zr-MOF UiO-66,[17] which consists of 12-connected hexanuclear zirconium (Zr6) nodes, for the degradation reaction of waste plastic PET (Figure 1) since UiO-66 is one of the most thermally and chemically stable MOFs known and can be easily synthesized both in high yields and on large scales, establishing its promise as a potential platform for industrial application.[15a, 18] Initial studies reveal that UiO-66 employed as a catalyst can effectively deconstruct both the model substrate 1,2-ethanediol dibenzoate (EDB) and PET in high yields under ${\sf H}_{2}$ or Ar. Further characterization studies reveal that during the PET degradation process, UiO-66 transforms into MIL-140A, which is another $z_{r}$ -MOF that also shows good catalytic activity under similar reaction conditions. Detailed mechanistic studies indicate that UiO-66 appears to catalytically degrade PET through both hydrogenolysis and $\upbeta$ -scission (thermolysis) processes. Finally, we observed that in some cases, the PET degradation product TA can exchange with functionalized linkers used in UiO-66 analogues. Overall, we believe this study will lay the groundwork for a broader application of MOF catalysts employed for waste plastic degradation.
## Results and Discussion
PET, PP and PE were cut from bottles; $5\mathrm{mol\%}$ UiO-66 and $Z r_{6}$ cluster; 3.75 $m o l\%$ MIL-140A; 10mg of $Z r O_{2}$ . [a] Reaction under $220^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $20{\mathsf{h}}$ . [b] Reaction under $260^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $24~\mathsf{h}$ . [c] Yields determined by $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectroscopy. Total yield for EDB reaction is benzoic acid yield, total yield for PET reaction is ${\mathsf{T A}}{+}{\mathsf{M M T}}$ yield. N.R. $=N O$ reaction.
First, we studied the catalytic reactivity of UiO-66 for transesterification and hydrogenation processes using EDB as a model substrate for polyester-based plastics (Scheme 1). We conducted the deconstruction reaction of EDB by heating the mixture of EDB and UiO-66 in a vial at $220^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under 1 atm ${\sf H}_{2}$ for 20 hours. The $^1\mathsf{H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectra obtained following the reaction (Figure S2A) confirm that the solid product, which sublimed from the bottom and crystallized at the top of the vial, is mainly benzoic acid (BA), with an isolated yield of $67\%$ (Table 1, entry 2). Then we also performed the reaction of EDB at $220^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under 1 atm Ar instead of ${\sf H}_{2}$ . Indeed, $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectroscopic analysis of the product (Figure S2B) confirms the formation of BA in $56\%$ yield under Ar (Table 1, entry 3). When heating EDB under the same conditions but without any catalyst present, no products were observed (Figure S3; Table 1, entry 1), indicating that EDB is thermally stable at $220^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for at least 20 h. Combined, these results demonstrate that UiO-66 can effectively deconstruct EDB.
Encouraged by the good catalytic activity of UiO-66 towards the deconstruction of EDB, we then investigated the catalytic activity of UiO-66 for actual polyester-based plastic PET degradation. We combined PET waste chips (from a plastic bottle, Figure 2A) and $5\mathrm{mol\%}$ UiO-66 powder (based on formula weight) in a vial and heated the reaction at $260~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under 1 atm ${\sf H}_{2}$ for $24\ h$ , the condition for the following reaction will be consistent as above unless otherwise specified. The $^1\mathsf{H}$ and $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ NMR spectra and electrospray ionization mass spectrometry (ESI-MS) results obtained following completion of the reaction confirm that the white solid products (Figure 2B) are TA and mono-methyl terephthalate (MMT) (Figure 2C, Figures S4-S6). The isolated yield profiles for both TA and MMT are shown in Figure 2D. Notably, the total TA $^+$ MMT yield of $\sim98\%$ after $24\ h$ indicates that the sample plastic is almost completely degraded under these conditions (Table 1, entry 4). When heating the plastic under Ar instead of ${\sf H}_{2}$ , we observed a total ${\mathsf{T A}}{+}{\mathsf{M M T}}$ yield of $81\%$ (Figure S7; Table 1, entry 5); in the absence of UiO-66 but under otherwise analogous conditions, we observed TA in about $24\%$ yield under ${\sf H}_{2}$ and $16\%$ yield under Ar, along with negligible MMT (Figure S8). The result is highly consistent with our EDB deconstruction experiment, showing that higher yield can be obtained with ${\sf H}_{2}$ . In general, when ${\sf H}_{2}$ is not present, esters and polyesters can thermally degrade via $\upbeta$ -scission under high temperatures (Scheme $2$ ).[19] And the higher yield under ${\sf H}_{2}$ indicates the presence of another pathway – likely hydrogenolysis – as observed in other studies[11-12]. We also explored the catalytic activity of zirconium oxide $(Z r O_{2})$ and the $Z r_{6}$ cluster[20], mimicking the $Z r_{6}$ node of UiO-66, under the same reaction conditions. TA formed in $27\%$ and $32\%$ yields when $Z r O_{2}$ and the $Z\mathsf{r}_{6}$ cluster were used, respectively, along with a $3\%$ yield of MMT in both cases (Figure S9; Table 1, entries 8 and entry 9). In addition, we also identified benzoic acid, which is the capping ligand coordinated to the $Z\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}}}_{6}$ cluster, in the $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectrum of the reaction product (Figure S9B), suggesting decomposition of the $Z\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}}}_{6}$ cluster under these conditions. Moreover, the amorphous powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern obtained after activating the $Z\mathsf{r}_{6}$ cluster at $250^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ corroborates this observation and indicates collapse of the structure (Figure S10). Taken together, these results demonstrate that the low catalytic activity using the Zr6 cluster could be due to the unstable nature under the reaction conditions, while less exposed Zr sites and/or structure defects might be the reason in term of using $Z\mathsf{r O}_{2}$ , further confirming the significance of using UiO-66 as a catalyst.
Next, we explored the effectiveness of this catalyst in the presence of additional mixed plastics of PET $^+$ polypropylene (PP) or PET $^+$ polyethylene (PE) (cut from a plastic bottle and a bottle cap; molar ratio $\mathsf{P E T:}(\mathsf{P P/P E})\ =\ 10:1)$ . Notably, UiO-66 remains unaffected by the presence of either PP or PE as we observed both high activity and selectivity for PET degradation, with total $\mathsf{T A}+\mathsf{M M T}$ yields of $91\%$ and $93\%$ , respectively (Figure S11; Table 1, entry 6 and entry 7). In addition, conducting the reaction with green- dyed PET plastic, rather than clear PET plastic, also resulted in a total ${\mathsf{T A}}{+}{\mathsf{M M T}}$ yield of $98\%$ ; importantly, the products were obtained as white solids (Figure S12), highlighting the effectiveness of the UiO-66 catalytic system towards the challenging degradation of dyed PET plastics. Combined, these encouraging results suggest that UiO-66 can effectively promote the degradation of PET.
We then characterized the UiO-66 sample after the catalytic reaction conducted under ${\sf H}_{2}$ to determine the stability of the catalyst under these reaction conditions. After thoroughly washes with N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) and acetone, the postcatalysis sample was activated at $150^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $12\mathsf{h}$ and subjected to powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analysis. The result reveals that while the post-catalysis UiO-66 sample retains crystallinity, noticeable changes in peak positions and intensities occur (Figure 3A). Specifically, in addition to the original peaks at $7.37^{\circ}$ and $8.52^{\circ}$ in the PXRD patterns for UiO-66, two new peaks appear at $7.45^{\circ}$ and $8.74^{\circ}$ , indicating partial changes in the structure of the catalyst. Moreover, the peaks of the post-catalysis UiO-66 are broadened, which indicates a decrease in the crystallite size for this sample after catalysis. Similar patterns are also observed for the post-catalysis UiO-66 obtained after conducting the reaction under Ar (Figure S13). Next, we examined the porosity of the post-catalysis UiO-66 material by obtaining ${\sf N}_{2}$ isotherms at $77\mathsf{K}$ (Figure 3B) and found that the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) area decreases from $1160\mathrm{m}^{2}/9$ for UiO-66 to $600~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ following degradation. While surface area decreases for MOFs following catalytic reactions are usually attributed to either clogging of the pores by the residual reagents and products or partial MOF degradation, scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images hinted at a potentially different explanation as they illustrate a change in morphology for the UiO-66 crystallites, which start as octahedra prior to the reaction and transform to rodlike shapes following catalysis (Figure 3C and 3D).
Intrigued by the changes observed by PXRD and SEM imaging, we sought a deeper understanding of the potential structural transformations that occur during catalysis. MIL-140A,[21] which is a Zr-MOF that contains sevenfold-coordinated zirconium oxide chains connected via TA to six other chains, contains the peaks in its PXRD patterns that match with the new peaks at $7.45^{\circ}$ and $8.74^{\circ}$ observed in the UiO-66 post-catalysis patterns (Figure 3A). Guillerm et al.[21-22] estimated that for the reaction between zirconium salts and TA, MIL-140A is the thermodynamic product, while the UiO-66 phase is the kinetic product. Peak fitting analysis shows that the PXRD patterns of the post-catalysis UiO-66 sample consist of both peak features for UiO-66 and MIL-140A (Figure S14). Additionally, we estimated the average crystallite sizes of these samples using the Scherrer equation and found that after catalysis, the UiO-66 crystallites decrease from about 67 nm to $24~\mathsf{n m}$ , which is comparable to the crystallite size of MIL-140A (32 nm); analysis of the SEM images for these samples reveals a similar trend of decreasing crystallite size after catalysis (Figure 3C-3E). We then considered the possibility that the initial UiO-66 catalyst transforms into MIL-140A (Figure 3E) during the degradation process. To probe this hypothesis, we employed transmission electron microscopy (TEM)[23]. The electron diffraction (ED) results for UiO-66 post-catalysis show two main patterns (Figure 3F and $3\mathsf{G})$ , which correspond to the two patterns from [110] and [101] zone axes of MIL-140A (Figure 3H and 3I). Based on the combined evidence from PXRD, SEM, and TEM, we believe that UiO-66 partially transforms into MIL-140A during the degradation reaction. Based on the formulas of UiO-66 and MIL-140A, which are $Z r_{6}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}({\mathsf{O H}})_{4}({\mathsf{O}}_{2}{\mathsf{C}}-{\mathsf{C}}_{6}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}-{\mathsf{C O}}_{2})_{6}$ for UiO-66 and $Z r_{8}{\mathsf O}_{8}({\mathsf O}_{2}{\mathsf C}{\mathsf-}{\mathsf C}_{6}{\mathsf H}_{4}{\mathsf C}{\mathsf O}_{2})_{8}$ for MIL-140A, the ratio for Zr:terephthalate is identical and 1:1 in both cases, indicating that the transformation of $\mathsf{U i O-}66$ to MIL-140A will not affect the calculation of the product’s yield. In addition, UiO-66 exposed to the same catalytic reaction conditions of $260^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and 1 atm ${\sf H}_{2}$ , but in the absence of PET or with equivalent TA, does not induce any structural or morphological changes as observed by PXRD and SEM imaging (Figure S16 and S17).
To investigate if the catalyst transformation product still exhibits catalytic reactivity, we then conducted the PET degradation reaction with $3.75\ m\%$ MIL-140A (based on formula weight, equivalent $Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm r}$ amount as $5\mathrm{\mol}\%$ UiO-66) under analogous conditions. Figure 2D illustrates the yield of TA and MMT as a function of reaction time, demonstrating that MIL-140A achieves a total $\mathsf{T A}+\mathsf{M M T}$ yield of $88\%$ after $24\ h$ under $H_{2}$ (compared to $98\%$ for UiO-66 under the same conditions), confirming good catalytic activity for MIL-140A towards PET degradation. In the absence of ${\sf H}_{2}$ , MIL-140A can also catalyze this transformation with a total $\mathsf{T A}+\mathsf{M M T}$ yield of $67\%$ (Figure S18B). Additionally, the selectivity of TA vs. MMT for MIL-140A is slightly higher than that of UiO-66 when the total yield surpasses $80\%$ (Figure 2E). PXRD patterns (Figure 3A), ${\sf N}_{2}$ isotherms (Figure 3B), and SEM images (Figure S19) confirm that MIL-140A is stable under the reaction conditions, though the surface area of MIL-140A postcatalysis slightly decreases from $455\ \mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ to $330~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ . And the lower catalytic activity of MIL-140A than UiO-66 might be due to the lower surface area and less exposed $Z r$ sites.
Considering that the main product from the reaction, TA, is also the organic linker for UiO-66, we speculated whether linker exchange might occur during the degradation process. To probe this possibility, we conducted PET degradation reactions with UiO-66-F and UiO-66-NH2, which feature the same $Z\boldsymbol{\mathsf{r}}$ -based node as in UiO-66 but with 2-fluoroterephthalic acid (TA-F) and 2- aminoterephthalic acid $(T A-N H_{2})$ linkers, respectively. PXRD patterns (Figure S21) indicate that post-catalysis UiO-66-F exhibits good crystallinity but also evolves to MIL-140A. Additionally, the $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectrum (Figure S22) of this reaction product reveals that approximately $80\%$ of TA-F from UiO-66-F is present in the product. Moreover, $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectroscopic analysis of the digested catalyst post-reaction corroborates this observation and indicates that TA is the predominant linker in the sample (Figure S23). SEM images obtained for the post-catalysis UiO-66-F sample illustrate a crystallite transformation from octahedral to rodlike shapes, which is consistent with the formation of MIL-140A (Figure S24). In contrast, $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectra obtained following the analogous reaction with UiO- $66\substack{-N H_{2}}$ do not show any obvious linker exchange from $T A-N H_{2}$ to TA (Figure S26). PXRD patterns indicate good agreement between the pristine and post-catalysis ${\mathsf{U i O}}{\cdot}66{\cdot}{\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ samples (Figure S27), implying that no noticeable structural changes occur under these conditions and the UiO-66-NH2 remain as octahedral particles after catalysis (Figure S28). These results suggest that TA-F linkers in UiO-66-F can be swapped by the PET degradation product TA during catalysis while the MOF crystals undergo a phase transition to MIL-140A (Figure 4B). In contrast, UiO-66- NH2 does not allow linker exchange with TA under identical conditions and remains in its original morphology and structure throughout the reaction (Figure 4C). These linker exchange phenomena among UiO-66, UiO-66-F, and $V_{1}O-66-N H_{2}$ are potentially rationalized by the different acid dissociation constant (pKa) values of the linkers. For example, the linker with higher pKa value will afford stronger linker-metal bonds in the MOF, which means the linker is more difficult to be exchanged.[24] The pKa values of TA, TA-F and TA- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ are predicted to be 3.49, 2.86 and 3.95, respectively, indicating that the TA-F can be exchanged by TA, while exchange of $T A-N H_{2}$ by TA is more difficult – especially under these conditions in which the concentration of the alternative linker is very low.
Figure 4. Illustrations of Structural phase transition and linker exchange phenomenon of the catalysts during the degradation process. (A) Structural phase transition of UiO-66. (B) Linker exchange and phase transition of UiO66-F. (C) No linker exchange and phase transition for UiO-66-NH2.
Unlike PET hydrogenolysis with molybdenum catalysts[11-12], which afford TA as the principal solid product, PET degradation catalyzed by UiO-66 forms two main solid products: TA and MMT. According to the previous studies,[25] MMT is considered to be a characteristic pyrolysis product of PET. To confirm if hydrogenolysis is also involved in the formation of MMT under these conditions, we first conducted the reaction in the presence of ${\sf D}_{2}$ instead of ${\sf H}_{2}$ . If the ${\sf H}_{2}\left({\sf D}_{2}\right)$ can break the C–C bond between two ester groups directly, which is extremely difficult, MMT with deuterated methyl (deuterated MMT) will form. However, $^1\mathsf{H}$ and $^2{\mathsf{H}}$ NMR spectra (Figure S29) obtained for the product of this reaction indicate the presence of protonated MMT with no peaks corresponding to deuterated MMT observed (Figure 5A), indicating that the hydrogens on the methyl group in MMT are not from ${\sf H}_{2}$ . Next, we conducted degradation reactions using deuterated poly (ethylene- ${\cdot}{\mathsf{d}}_{4}$ terephthalate) ( ${\dot{\mathsf{d}}}_{4}$ -PET, Figure 5A) under Ar. The ESI-MS spectrum of the product (Figure S30) suggests the formation of deuterated MMT under these conditions. However, $^1\mathsf{H}$ and $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ NMR spectra of the product (Figure S31) show that not all hydrogens on the methyl group are replaced by deuterium, but rather the H:D ratio is about 1:2 (Figure 5A). Since we did not rigorously exclude water from the system prior to this reaction, we hypothesize that this hydrogen comes from the small amount of water present in the reaction (either from humidity or the PET). When this reaction with ${\mathsf{d}}_{4}$ -PET is performed under ${\sf H}_{2}$ rather than Ar, there is no apparent change in the H to D ratio for the methyl group of MMT relative to that of the product from the reaction performed under Ar (Figure S32), confirming that $H_{2}$ does not directly participate in the formation of MMT. In addition, we collected the headspace gas in the vial after the reaction and analyzed it using gas chromatography–mass spectrometry (GCMS), and we observed the presence of ethylene, water, and acetaldehyde (Figure S33). Previous studies[25b, 26] have shown that similar side products can be generated through PET thermolysis when PET is heated at a high temperature $(>300^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ . The small improvement in TA yield under ${\sf H}_{2}$ compared to Ar shows the limited effectiveness of UiO-66 in hydrogen activation. Combined, these results suggest that the major pathway involves UiO-66 effectively catalyzing the $\upbeta$ -scission process, allowing PET thermolysis to proceed at lower temperatures, while hydrogenolysis might be the minor reaction pathway since the overall TA yield improves when the reaction is performed under ${\sf H}_{2}$ rather than Ar.
Remarkably, UiO-66 does not decompose after the degradation reaction but rather evolves into a more robust framework in MIL140A that still possesses good catalytic reactivity for PET degradation. We conducted four consecutive experiments using UiO-66 as the catalyst to assess the recyclability of the catalyst and the robustness of the system. After each reaction, the solid product that sublimed to the top of the vial was removed for NMR spectroscopic analysis, and the same amount of fresh PET was added into the vial to start the next run. Although we observed a significant loss after the first run due to the transformation of UiO66 to MIL-140A, the yields of the $2^{\mathsf{n d}}$ , $3^{\mathsf{r d}}$ , and $4^{\mathrm{th}}$ runs were steady (with an average TA $^+$ MMT yield of $74\%$ in the last 3 runs), confirming that this catalyst retains most of the reactivity after at least 4 consecutive runs (Figure S34).
## Conclusion
In this work, we demonstrate that the Zr-MOF UiO-66 can effectively deconstruct PET into the monomers terephthalic acid (TA) and mono-methyl terephthalate (MMT) with total yields of $98\%$ under ${\sf H}_{2}$ and $81\%$ under $\overline{{\mathsf{A}\mathsf{r}}}$ in 24 hours. In contrast to other catalytic systems used for PET hydrogenolysis, UiO-66 exhibits good catalytic reactivity under Ar, demonstrating its propensity to promote the $\upbeta$ -scission of PET rather than hydrogenolysis. Furthermore, mechanistic studies suggest that this catalytic system engages in the $\upbeta$ -scission process as the major PET degradation pathway when the reaction is performed under either Ar or ${\sf H}_{2}$ , while hydrogenolysis proceeds as a minor pathway when the reaction is performed in an atmosphere of $H_{2}$ . Moreover, this PET degradation reaction is not affected by the presence of polyethylene and polypropylene contaminants in the system, and the degradation of dyed PET affords white products. During the PET degradation process, we observed that UiO-66 transforms into MIL-140A, a related Zr-MOF built from the same Zr6 node and TA linker, and that MIL-140A is still capable of catalyzing the same transformation. In addition, TA produced from PET degradation during the reaction can exchange with the fluorinated TA linkers in the analogue UiO-66-F. While the nature of the phase transition phenomenon and the reaction mechanism still require further investigation, we believe these findings have the potential to broaden the application of MOF catalysts and will serve as guidance for the design of next-generation polymer degradation catalysts for achieving a circular polymer economy.
